P/E measures how much investors pay for each dollar of earnings and is a common benchmark to quickly evaluate
companies within the same industry. Price per share is the cost of a single share of stock on the market. A 
P/E multiple tells you same thing as the P/E ratio but P/E is measured in dollars while the P/E multiple is 
ratio of price to earnings (i.e. 20x multiple if a stock trades at $100 and earns $5 per share). It is 
difficult to compare total earnings alone because companies have different sizes; by using the P/E ratio, 
you can normalize the comparison. P/E is useful in conjunction with a DCF because it adds a market-based
comparison to the value of the DCF. Whereas a DCF estimates what a company should be worth based on future
cash flows, the P/E ratio shows how the market values a company's earnings compared to companies within
the same industry. P/E can be useful when comparing business models and industries, comparing the same company
throughout its history, comparing profit margins, and more; however, P/E can also be misleading. When earnings 
are negative, P/E is negative and therefore useless. Similarily, one-time gains, restructuring costs, tax 
benefits, bail outs, or similar circumstances can give the ratio a distorted price because it only factors in
1 year of earnings. In addition, different growth projections (fast vs slow growth) and different debt levels 
(high debt or interest rates can distort the P/E) are also highly influential and can change the P/E. Therefore,
a lower P/E is not always a good investment. 

